Argument in Favor of Measure D
Vote YES on Measure D to Bring Fairer Elections to Irvine
Shouldn’t “majority rule” govern our elections? Yet in Irvine, candidates
are regularly elected with less than one-third of the vote. Which means
two-thirds of voters are represented by someone they don’t want. That is
NOT real democracy.
Ranked Choice Voting (RCV) guarantees that Irvine residents elect
officeholders who win majority support. And it’s cost-effective. THIS is
what real democracy looks like.
Why Irvine Needs Measure D:
• Guarantees Majority Support: RCV acts as an “instant runoff.”
Voters rank their favorite candidates in order of preference: 1, 2,
3, and so on. If no candidate receives over 50% of first-choice
rankings, the last-place candidate is eliminated, and their votes
transfer to each voter’s second choice. This continues until a
candidate wins a majority.
• More Choice, More Voice: Never again will you have to vote for
the “lesser evil.” RCV allows you to vote for your favorite candidate
first, knowing your vote will still count toward your backup choice
if your first choice loses.
• Rewards Positive, Solution-Focused Campaigns: Because
candidates often need second and third choice rankings to
win, negative campaigning backfires. Instead, RCV incentivizes
positive, issue-oriented campaigns that unite rather than divide
the community.
• Protects Taxpayers: This measure ensures fiscal accountability
with a strict safeguard: RCV will only be implemented if the cost is
below 0.23% of the city’s budget. This responsibly modernizes our
democracy while protecting your tax dollars.
Irvine deserves a democracy where the majority rules, candidates focus
on solutions rather than negative attacks, and voters don’t have to settle
for the “lesser evil.” Measure D delivers exactly that. Join dozens of cities
in California and across the US using RCV. More choice and a stronger
voice! Vote YES on D.
Learn more at VoterChoiceIrvine.org
s/ Kathleen Treseder
Councilmember
Rebuttal to Argument in Favor of Measure D
THE “SPENDING CAP” IS A SHAM. The number moves around —
it’s a percentage of the city’s massive general fund, and could eventually
rise to $1.5 million, $2 million or more. We don’t know. We’re just
guesstimating. And the only cost estimates come from the group pushing
you to vote yes. Irvine can’t afford that kind of surprise: the city faces a $6
million deficit this year and a $47 million shortfall ahead.
NO OTHER CITY IN ORANGE COUNTY USES THIS TODAY. The
Orange County Registrar admits in writing that it has no certified system
for implementing ranked-choice voting — that’s why no infrastructure
exists, and Irvine would bear the massive startup costs.
NOT BROKEN. NOT NEEDED. Irvine has never held a runoff election.
This “fix” solves a problem we don’t have.
“DOZENS OF CITIES”? LOOK WHAT HAPPENED THERE.
Implementation problems have surfaced across the country — including
in Oakland, one of the flagship cities RCV advocates point to as proof it
works. In 2022, a misconfigured algorithm led Alameda County to certify
the wrong winner in an Oakland school board race. The certified winner
was declared the loser weeks later, once the error was corrected. New
York’s 2021 mayoral primary saw the same kind of chaos, when 135,000
test ballots were mistakenly counted.
Our elections work now. Implementing this will be very
expensive. And it has had some serious problems in some places
where it has been tried.
VOTE NO on Measure D.
s/James Mai
Vice Mayor
